The Grow Zambia Agenda has moved from inauguration rhetoric to the machinery of government: a K284.8 billion budget proposal, realigned ministries, and ministers already cracking the whip on performance. Meanwhile, the President returned to the campaign trail in Mandevu to say thank you — and to make new promises. Here is what the reporting delivers, and where it still falls short.

## Main Stories

1. **Government proposes K284.8 billion Budget for 2027** — Finance Minister Situmbeko Musokotwane presented a K284.8 billion budget, with 82.1 percent (K233.7 billion) from domestic revenue, K41.9 billion borrowing and K9.2 billion in grants. Key measures include raising withholding tax on mining dividends from zero to 15 percent and reforming the minimum alternative tax to apply only after three consecutive loss-making years. The reporting gives headline figures but no sector-by-sector allocations — the detail that determines whether Grow Zambia is financed or merely announced. Watch for the mining houses' response to the dividend tax change, which is absent here.

2. **Maize means money: Why 10m tonnes will feed, fund and employ Zambia** — A detailed explainer on the 10·10·5·3·3·1·1·1 targets, noting current maize production of 3–4 million tonnes in a good year, with the President claiming a record 5.1 million tonnes last season. The piece argues 10 million tonnes would guarantee food security and dollar earnings, and references Parliament's October 27 vote approving 30 realigned ministries. It is persuasive advocacy rather than neutral reporting — there is no costing, no land or water availability analysis, and no comment from farmers' associations or independent agronomists on whether the target is achievable.

3. **Grow Zambia: Musokotwane's biggest agenda for 2027 budget** — This preview frames the budget as the test of the Grow Zambia blueprint, recalling the 2026 budget's K253.1 billion (27.4 percent of GDP), with K92.6 billion to general public services, K58.6 billion to economic affairs, K33 billion to education and K26.2 billion to health. It asks the right question — how much will government spend, and will it translate into results — but answers it only with the 2026 figures. The 2027 allocations are the story, and they are not yet in this report.

4. **Nyambose mulls monthly performance targets** — Labour Minister Misheck Nyambose told ministry staff that Grow Zambia targets are "non-negotiable," directing monthly performance targets, unannounced office visits and warnings against corruption. The rhetoric is firm, but the article offers no baseline data on current ministry performance, no indication of what happens to underperformers beyond "change now before you are changed," and no detail on how monthly reports will be verified rather than self-reported.

5. **Thank you Zambia!** — At his first post-election rally in Mandevu, President Hichilema thanked voters, promised action on water, roads and security, and introduced new MP Adrian Banda — declared winner after three opposition candidates withdrew from a by-election scheduled for November 5. That walkover deserves more scrutiny than it gets here; the report does not say why the candidates pulled out. The President also directed security wings to transform "junkies" into responsible citizens through skills training, a pledge with no timeline or programme detail.

6. **Wonderful Group seeks $1.2bn railway investment** — The company wants to rehabilitate the Livingstone–Chililabombwe railway line to shift cargo from road to rail, with Deputy Secretary to the Cabinet Siazongo Siakalenge noting the firm runs about 1,000 trucks daily. Siakalenge himself flagged that industrial park status took eight months to grant — an admission of administrative drag that sits awkwardly beside the government's investment-friendly messaging. No financing agreement, no start date, and no confirmation the investment is committed rather than proposed.

## Other Notable Stories

**Economy & Investment:**
* The 2027 budget's domestic revenue share is projected to rise from 58.7 percent in 2021 to 82.1 percent in 2027 — an impressive trajectory, but the report does not address whether taxpayers can bear it or how compliance will be enforced.
* The mining dividend withholding tax change is the budget's most consequential measure for the copper sector; no revenue estimate is given for what it will raise.

**Governance & Public Service:**
* The realignment of 30 ministries, approved by Parliament on October 27, is the administrative backbone of Grow Zambia — but the reporting offers no analysis of whether the new structure duplicates functions or how much it costs to run.

**Education & Recognition:**
* Dr Maariyah Desai (UNZA) and Mr Luyando Linyama (ZCAS University) have been named Zambia's 2027 Rhodes Scholars-Elect and will head to Oxford in October 2027. A genuine point of national pride, and a reminder that the country's talent pipeline extends well beyond its current economic constraints.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* The 2027 budget's sector allocations are the accountability test: watch for the detailed breakdown to see whether agriculture, mining, energy and manufacturing receive the funding the Grow Zambia targets require, or whether debt service continues to crowd them out.
* Mining's response to the 15 percent dividend withholding tax and the minimum alternative tax reform will shape investment sentiment — track statements from the Chamber of Mines and whether any operators signal reduced capital expenditure.
* The Mandevu by-election walkover (November 5) and the President's "junkies" directive both need follow-up: why did three opposition candidates withdraw, and what concrete programme emerges from the skills-training pledge?